      "topic_id": "bearings_cosine_rule",
      "prompt": "A ship sails from port A on a bearing of 307° for 14 km to point B. It then changes course and sails on a bearing of 209° for 24 km to point C. Find the direct distance from A to C, correct to 1 decimal place.",
      "solution_steps": [
        "The back bearing of A from B is 307° + 180° = 127°.",
        "Angle ABC is the difference between this back bearing and the bearing of C from B: angle ABC = 82°.",
        "Use the cosine rule: AC² = AB² + BC² - 2×AB×BC×cos(ABC)",
        "AC² = 14² + 24² - 2×14×24×cos(82°) = 678.5",
        "AC = √678.5 = 26.0 km (1 d.p.)"
      ],
      "final_answer": "26.0 km",
